  <Properties>
    <blocks>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "contentData": [
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "e0876897-88f4-4dcf-ae0a-ec5a37e3a185",
      "key": "a867a606-cbb5-45ed-ab7b-9d25839efad0",
      "udi": null,
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "text",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "Layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp class=\u0022govuk-body\u0022\u003EThe GOV.UK Design System recommends using pagination differently depending on how many pages there are. A pagination partial view expects a\u00A0\u003Cem\u003EPaginationModel\u003C/em\u003E\u00A0and applies this and other pagination logic for you. See the Razor source of this page for an example.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp class=\u0022govuk-body\u0022\u003EThere are behaviours not covered by the partial view which you should implement:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul class=\u0022govuk-list govuk-list--bullet\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003ERedirect users to the first page if they enter a URL of a page that no longer exists.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003EShow the page number in the page \u0026lt;title\u0026gt; so that screen reader users know they\u2019ve navigated to a different page. For example, \u2018Search results (page 1 of 4)\u2019.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "bfeed15c-3ebb-46d4-b805-07d0f9b083c7",
